\title{Oracle Reductions and Composition}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    [ArkLib](dhsorens-0040)'s organising abstraction, and the reason it can hope to cover many protocols rather than one. A reduction maps one relation to another; a protocol is a composition of reductions; completeness and soundness are proved once per component.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{The definition: relations with oracle statements, and what a reduction between them is.}
        \li{Sequential composition, and [how the soundness error accounts](dhsorens-004N).}
        \li{Lifting and virtual oracle reductions (\code{LiftContext}) — why real protocols need a component to run in a larger context than it was stated in.}
        \li{Decomposing a full system this way. [WHIR](dhsorens-003C) or [Spartan](dhsorens-003G) is the test case.}
        \li{What is lost: which protocols resist a clean decomposition, and why.}
    }
}

\p{
    Sources: [ArkLib](https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib), [ArkLib on lean-lang.org](https://lean-lang.org/use-cases/arklib/).
}
